// NEGATIVE TEST -- a TYPE may not take a built-in's name either (N14-02), and
// this fixture exists because the REASON is a different one.
//
// Nothing breaks in the emitted C here, and that was measured, not assumed:
// `struct print_state { n: i64 }` renamed to `print_int` compiled, linked, ran
// and printed `ok`. A built-in mangles to `__pd_print_int` and a struct emits
// `struct print_int`, so C has two names and no collision. The C compiler has
// no objection to make.
//
// It is refused on the LANGUAGE's rule instead: one namespace for top-level
// names, the same rule that already refuses `const Meters` beside
// `type Meters` (tests/reject/const_collides_with_type_alias.pd) with no C
// collision either. A reader who meets `print_int` in this program cannot tell
// whether it is the built-in or the struct without checking the registry.
//
// So the diagnostic must not claim a collision that does not exist, and the
// fingerprint below is on the DECISION sentence rather than the headline --
// the headline is shared with the function and local positions, and a fixture
// pinned to it would stay green if this carve-out silently became a
// C-collision claim.
struct print_int {
    n: i64,
}

fn main() {
    print("ok");
}
